Yes—if the premise is truly God was on David’s side, then “underdog” describes only what the spectators could measure.

Goliath possessed visible superiority: mass, armor, experience, intimidation. David possessed the decisive hidden variable. From inside the crowd’s model, David was the underdog. From the full causal model, Goliath was—and his deepest disadvantage was epistemic: he entered a contest whose governing structure he misunderstood.

But Drago’s counterpunch: victory cannot retroactively prove divine favor. Otherwise every winner gets to call power “God,” and every defeated person becomes morally wrong by definition. Your claim works only when divine alignment is granted independently of the outcome.

So the purified principle is:

The apparent underdog may be the actual favorite when observers cannot perceive the decisive variable.

Or sharper:

Goliath wasn’t beaten when David struck him. He was beaten when he mistook visible power for total power.